---
title: PulseAudio Compat
order: 3
description: pactl and pacmd via PipeWire compatibility layer
tags: [audio, pulseaudio, pactl, pacmd, pipewire-pulse]
---

PipeWire provides a PulseAudio compatibility layer (pipewire-pulse) so
existing PulseAudio tools and applications work without modification.
pactl is the most commonly used tool for audio management.

@code bash
# Verify PipeWire is serving as PulseAudio
pactl info | grep "Server Name"
# Output: Server Name: PulseAudio (on PipeWire x.y.z)

# Ensure pipewire-pulse is installed
sudo pacman -S pipewire-pulse

@text
Listing sinks (outputs), sources (inputs), and streams.

@code bash
# List audio outputs (sinks)
pactl list sinks short
pactl list sinks                 # detailed info

# List audio inputs (sources)
pactl list sources short
pactl list sources               # detailed info

# List active playback streams
pactl list sink-inputs short

# List active recording streams
pactl list source-outputs short

# List available cards
pactl list cards short

# List loaded modules
pactl list modules short

@text
Volume control with pactl. Sink/source can be specified by index number
or name.

@code bash
# Set sink volume (output)
pactl set-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ 50%      # set to 50%
pactl set-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ +5%      # increase by 5%
pactl set-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ -5%      # decrease by 5%
pactl set-sink-volume 0 80%                   # by index

# Mute/unmute sink
pactl set-sink-mute @DEFAULT_SINK@ toggle
pactl set-sink-mute @DEFAULT_SINK@ 1          # mute
pactl set-sink-mute @DEFAULT_SINK@ 0          # unmute

# Set source volume (input/microphone)
pactl set-source-volume @DEFAULT_SOURCE@ 80%
pactl set-source-mute @DEFAULT_SOURCE@ toggle

# Set volume on a specific stream
pactl set-sink-input-volume <stream-id> 60%
pactl set-sink-input-mute <stream-id> toggle

@text
Setting defaults and moving streams between devices.

@code bash
# Set default output sink
pactl set-default-sink <sink-name-or-id>

# Set default input source
pactl set-default-source <source-name-or-id>

# Move a running stream to a different sink
pactl move-sink-input <stream-id> <sink-id>

# Move a recording stream to a different source
pactl move-source-output <stream-id> <source-id>

# Get current default sink
pactl get-default-sink

# Get current default source
pactl get-default-source

@text
Card profile management for switching between output modes
(e.g., headphones vs speakers, analog vs HDMI).

@code bash
# List card profiles
pactl list cards | grep -A 20 "Profiles:"

# Set card profile
pactl set-card-profile <card-id> output:analog-stereo
pactl set-card-profile <card-id> output:hdmi-stereo
pactl set-card-profile <card-id> off         # disable card

# Common profiles:
#   output:analog-stereo
#   output:analog-surround-51
#   output:hdmi-stereo
#   input:analog-stereo
#   output:analog-stereo+input:analog-stereo

@text
Monitoring and event subscription.

@code bash
# Monitor all PulseAudio events
pactl subscribe
# Useful for debugging device hotplug, stream creation, volume changes

# One-shot monitoring with callback
pactl subscribe | while read line; do
  echo "$(date): $line"
done

@text
pacmd provides an interactive shell. With PipeWire's compatibility
layer, most commands work but some internals differ from real PulseAudio.

@code bash
# Interactive shell (limited under PipeWire)
pacmd

# Common pacmd commands that work under pipewire-pulse
pacmd list-sinks
pacmd list-sources
pacmd list-sink-inputs
pacmd stat                       # server statistics
pacmd info                       # server info
pacmd dump                       # dump current configuration

# Note: pacmd set-* commands are deprecated in favor of pactl
# Some pacmd internals (e.g., module loading) may not work
# identically under PipeWire -- prefer pactl or wpctl instead

@text
Useful pactl recipes for scripting and keybindings.

@code bash
# Toggle mute (good for keyboard shortcut)
pactl set-sink-mute @DEFAULT_SINK@ toggle

# Volume up/down (good for media keys)
pactl set-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ +5%
pactl set-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ -5%

# Get current volume as percentage
pactl get-sink-volume @DEFAULT_SINK@ | grep -oP '\d+%' | head -1

# Check if muted
pactl get-sink-mute @DEFAULT_SINK@

# Play a sound notification
paplay /usr/share/sounds/freedesktop/stereo/bell.oga
